---
import ResourcePage from '@components/ResourcePage.astro'
import { Heading, Link } from 'accessible-astro-components'
---

<ResourcePage
  title="Social Security Disability Frequently Asked Questions"
  description="Find short answers to common questions about SSI, SSDI, disability applications, denials, hearings, evidence, and legal help."
  intro="Start with the question that sounds most like yours. These answers are general and may not fit every case."
  related={[
    { href: '/ssi-vs-ssdi', label: 'Compare SSI and SSDI' },
    { href: '/disability-appeals', label: 'Learn about denied claims' },
    { href: '/disability-glossary', label: 'Look up disability terms' },
  ]}
>
  <!-- FAQ list: native details controls work with keyboards and assistive technology. -->
  <Heading level="h2">Common questions</Heading>
  <div class="faq-list">
    <details>
      <summary>What is the difference between SSI and SSDI?</summary>
      <p>
        SSDI is generally connected to your covered work history. SSI is generally connected to limited income and
        resources. Both use Social Security’s disability rules. <Link href="/ssi-vs-ssdi">See the full comparison</Link
        >.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I apply for both programs?</summary>
      <p>
        Some people may apply for or receive benefits under both programs. Social Security reviews the rules and your
        facts.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How do I start an application?</summary>
      <p>
        Depending on the program, you may start online, by telephone, or with help from a Social Security office. <Link
          href="/applying-for-disability">Read the application guide</Link
        >.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What if Social Security denied my claim?</summary>
      <p>
        You may be able to appeal. Read the notice promptly because deadlines apply. <Link href="/disability-appeals"
          >Learn about an appeal</Link
        >.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Should I file a new application after a denial?</summary>
      <p>
        Do not assume a new application is the right next step. Learn how a new filing could affect an available appeal
        before you act.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What happens at a disability hearing?</summary>
      <p>
        A judge may ask about your medical conditions, work, treatment, and daily activities. Other witnesses may also
        speak. <Link href="/disability-hearings">Read the hearing guide</Link>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What records may help?</summary>
      <p>
        Social Security may need medical, work, and daily-life information. Keep notices and a list of your providers. <Link
          href="/disability-claim-evidence">Review evidence and documents</Link
        >.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does sending the contact form make the firm my lawyer?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Sending the demo form does not create an attorney-client relationship. The firm must review the matter and
        agree to represent you.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</ResourcePage>
